var e={domain:`frontend`,slug:`react`,title:`React`,order:3,summary:`Hooks, effects, patterns and the everyday React questions.`,questions:[{id:`react-props-vs-state`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Props vs state?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`state`,`props`],explain:"Props are passed in by the parent and are read-only for the child. State is data a component owns and updates itself (via `useState`/`useReducer`), and updating it triggers a re-render.",hook:`🎁 Props are gifts you're handed; state is what's in your own pocket.`,html:{explain:`<p>Props are passed in by the parent and are read-only for the child. State is data a component owns and updates itself (via <code>useState</code>/<code>useReducer</code>), and updating it triggers a re-render.</p>`,hook:`<p>🎁 Props are gifts you're handed; state is what's in your own pocket.</p>`}},{id:`react-rules-of-hooks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What are the rules of hooks, and why do they exist?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`hooks`],explain:`Call hooks only at the top level of a component or custom hook: never inside loops, conditions or nested functions, and never from plain JS functions. React tracks hooks by **call order** between renders, not by name, so a conditional hook shifts the order and desyncs its internal state.`,hook:`📚 React reads your hooks like a numbered list; skip one line and every answer after it lands in the wrong box.`,example:"```jsx\n// ❌ the order changes when `user` is missing\nif (user) { const [x, setX] = useState(0); }\n// ✅ always call it; branch inside\nconst [x, setX] = useState(0);\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Call hooks only at the top level of a component or custom hook: never inside loops, conditions or nested functions, and never from plain JS functions. React tracks hooks by <strong>call order</strong> between renders, not by name, so a conditional hook shifts the order and desyncs its internal state.</p>`,hook:`<p>📚 React reads your hooks like a numbered list; skip one line and every answer after it lands in the wrong box.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-comment">// ❌ the order changes when \`user\` is missing</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user) { <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [x, setX] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>); }
<span class="hljs-comment">// ✅ always call it; branch inside</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [x, setX] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>);
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-immutability`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Why must state be immutable?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`state`,`immutability`],explain:`React compares state by reference. If you mutate an object and set the same reference, React sees no change and skips the render. Always create new objects or arrays.`,hook:`🪞 React only notices a new mirror, not a smudge on the old one.`,example:"```js\nsetTodos(todos.map(t => t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t)); // ✅ new array\ntodos[0].done = true; setTodos(todos);                                 // ❌ same reference\n```",senior:`The flip side is useful: returning the *identical* object from a reducer on a no-op means nothing downstream re-renders.`,html:{explain:`<p>React compares state by reference. If you mutate an object and set the same reference, React sees no change and skips the render. Always create new objects or arrays.</p>`,hook:`<p>🪞 React only notices a new mirror, not a smudge on the old one.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-js hljs"><span class="hljs-title function_">setTodos</span>(todos.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">t</span> =></span> t.<span class="hljs-property">id</span> === id ? { ...t, <span class="hljs-attr">done</span>: !t.<span class="hljs-property">done</span> } : t)); <span class="hljs-comment">// ✅ new array</span>
todos[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>].<span class="hljs-property">done</span> = <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>; <span class="hljs-title function_">setTodos</span>(todos);                                 <span class="hljs-comment">// ❌ same reference</span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`,senior:`<p>The flip side is useful: returning the <em>identical</em> object from a reducer on a no-op means nothing downstream re-renders.</p>`}},{id:`react-useeffect`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useEffect`: dependencies, cleanup and common mistakes?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`hooks`,`effects`],explain:`It runs after paint whenever a dependency changes; its cleanup runs before the next run and on unmount. Common mistakes: missing dependencies (stale values), objects or functions as dependencies (it runs every render), no cleanup (leaks, race conditions), and using an effect for derived state that should simply be computed during render.`,hook:`🧹 An effect is a guest: it arrives after the party starts (paint), and must clean up before the next one or when the house closes.`,example:"```jsx\n// ❌ effect just to derive state\nuseEffect(() => setFullName(first + ' ' + last), [first, last]);\n// ✅ compute during render\nconst fullName = first + ' ' + last;\n```",html:{explain:`<p>It runs after paint whenever a dependency changes; its cleanup runs before the next run and on unmount. Common mistakes: missing dependencies (stale values), objects or functions as dependencies (it runs every render), no cleanup (leaks, race conditions), and using an effect for derived state that should simply be computed during render.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧹 An effect is a guest: it arrives after the party starts (paint), and must clean up before the next one or when the house closes.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-comment">// ❌ effect just to derive state</span>
<span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setFullName</span>(first + <span class="hljs-string">' '</span> + last), [first, last]);
<span class="hljs-comment">// ✅ compute during render</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> fullName = first + <span class="hljs-string">' '</span> + last;
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-fetch-race`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"How do you fetch data in `useEffect` without race conditions?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`effects`,`data-fetching`,`async`],explain:`Abort or ignore the old request in the cleanup, so a slow old response can't overwrite a newer one. Better still, use a data library (TanStack Query, SWR) or fetch in a Server Component.`,hook:`🏁 When the query changes, cancel the old runner before the new one starts, or the slow one finishes last and "wins".`,example:"```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const ctrl = new AbortController();\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${q}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })\n    .then(r => r.json()).then(setResults)\n    .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e); });\n  return () => ctrl.abort();\n}, [q]);\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Abort or ignore the old request in the cleanup, so a slow old response can't overwrite a newer one. Better still, use a data library (TanStack Query, SWR) or fetch in a Server Component.</p>`,hook:`<p>🏁 When the query changes, cancel the old runner before the new one starts, or the slow one finishes last and "wins".</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> ctrl = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">AbortController</span>();
  <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(<span class="hljs-string">\`/api/search?q=<span class="hljs-subst">\${q}</span>\`</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">signal</span>: ctrl.<span class="hljs-property">signal</span> })
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">r</span> =></span> r.<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>()).<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(setResults)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">e</span> =></span> { <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (e.<span class="hljs-property">name</span> !== <span class="hljs-string">'AbortError'</span>) <span class="hljs-title function_">setError</span>(e); });
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-function">() =></span> ctrl.<span class="hljs-title function_">abort</span>();
}, [q]);
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-strictmode`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Why does StrictMode run effects twice in development?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`effects`,`debugging`],explain:`It mounts, unmounts and remounts components to surface effects without proper cleanup. If running twice breaks something, the effect isn't idempotent. This only happens in development.`,hook:`🧪 StrictMode is a stress test: plug it in, pull it out, plug it in. Good effects don't flinch.`,html:{explain:`<p>It mounts, unmounts and remounts components to surface effects without proper cleanup. If running twice breaks something, the effect isn't idempotent. This only happens in development.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧪 StrictMode is a stress test: plug it in, pull it out, plug it in. Good effects don't flinch.</p>`}},{id:`react-controlled-uncontrolled`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Controlled vs uncontrolled inputs?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`forms`],explain:"**Controlled**: React state is the source of truth, updated via `onChange`; use it when you validate or derive UI from the value. **Uncontrolled**: the DOM holds the value and you read it with a ref or `FormData`; simpler for submit-only forms and file inputs.",hook:`🎮 Controlled = React holds the joystick; uncontrolled = the input drives itself and you check where it ended up.`,example:'```jsx\n<input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />           // controlled\n<form onSubmit={e => { const data = new FormData(e.currentTarget); }}>   // uncontrolled\n  <input name="email" />\n</form>\n```',html:{explain:`<p><strong>Controlled</strong>: React state is the source of truth, updated via <code>onChange</code>; use it when you validate or derive UI from the value. <strong>Uncontrolled</strong>: the DOM holds the value and you read it with a ref or <code>FormData</code>; simpler for submit-only forms and file inputs.</p>`,hook:`<p>🎮 Controlled = React holds the joystick; uncontrolled = the input drives itself and you check where it ended up.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs">&#x3C;input value={email} onChange={<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">e</span> =></span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setEmail</span>(e.<span class="hljs-property">target</span>.<span class="hljs-property">value</span>)} />           <span class="hljs-comment">// controlled</span>
<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">form</span> <span class="hljs-attr">onSubmit</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{e</span> =></span> { const data = new FormData(e.currentTarget); }}>   // uncontrolled
  <span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">input</span> <span class="hljs-attr">name</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"email"</span> /></span>
<span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">form</span>></span></span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-custom-hooks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What's a custom hook? Give an example.`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`hooks`,`reuse`],explain:"A function starting with `use` that composes other hooks to share **stateful logic**, not state itself. Each component that calls it gets its own independent state.",hook:`🧩 A custom hook is a recipe, not a shared pot: everyone cooks their own portion.`,example:`\`\`\`jsx
function useOnline() {
  const [online, setOnline] = useState(navigator.onLine);
  useEffect(() => {
    const on = () => setOnline(true), off = () => setOnline(false);
    addEventListener('online', on); addEventListener('offline', off);
    return () => { removeEventListener('online', on); removeEventListener('offline', off); };
  }, []);
  return online;
}
\`\`\``,html:{explain:`<p>A function starting with <code>use</code> that composes other hooks to share <strong>stateful logic</strong>, not state itself. Each component that calls it gets its own independent state.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧩 A custom hook is a recipe, not a shared pot: everyone cooks their own portion.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">useOnline</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [online, setOnline] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>(navigator.<span class="hljs-property">onLine</span>);
  <span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">on</span> = (<span class="hljs-params"></span>) => <span class="hljs-title function_">setOnline</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">true</span>), off = <span class="hljs-function">() =></span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setOnline</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">false</span>);
    <span class="hljs-title function_">addEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'online'</span>, on); <span class="hljs-title function_">addEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'offline'</span>, off);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-function">() =></span> { <span class="hljs-title function_">removeEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'online'</span>, on); <span class="hljs-title function_">removeEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'offline'</span>, off); };
  }, []);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> online;
}
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-memoisation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`: when do you use them?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`performance`,`memoisation`,`hooks`],explain:"`memo` skips re-rendering a component when its props are shallow-equal. `useMemo` caches a computed value; `useCallback` caches a function's identity. Use them when the Profiler shows a real cost, or when reference stability matters (an effect dependency, a prop to a memoised child), not by default. The React Compiler now does most of this automatically.",hook:`💾 Memoisation is insurance: worth it for expensive things, a waste of premiums on cheap ones.`,senior:'Say "I don\'t reach for these by default; I reach for them when the Profiler shows a problem." Inline objects and arrow functions passed as props are what usually break `memo`.',html:{explain:`<p><code>memo</code> skips re-rendering a component when its props are shallow-equal. <code>useMemo</code> caches a computed value; <code>useCallback</code> caches a function's identity. Use them when the Profiler shows a real cost, or when reference stability matters (an effect dependency, a prop to a memoised child), not by default. The React Compiler now does most of this automatically.</p>`,hook:`<p>💾 Memoisation is insurance: worth it for expensive things, a waste of premiums on cheap ones.</p>`,senior:`<p>Say "I don't reach for these by default; I reach for them when the Profiler shows a problem." Inline objects and arrow functions passed as props are what usually break <code>memo</code>.</p>`}},{id:`react-context`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Context: when do you use it, and what's the catch?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`context`,`state`,`performance`],explain:`Context suits low-frequency global values: theme, auth user, locale. The catch is that **every consumer re-renders** when the provider's value reference changes, whatever part it reads. Fix it by splitting contexts, memoising the value, or using a selector-based store for frequently changing data.`,hook:`📢 Context is a loudspeaker: every listener hears every announcement, even ones not meant for them.`,example:"```jsx\nconst value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user]); // stable reference\n<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Context suits low-frequency global values: theme, auth user, locale. The catch is that <strong>every consumer re-renders</strong> when the provider's value reference changes, whatever part it reads. Fix it by splitting contexts, memoising the value, or using a selector-based store for frequently changing data.</p>`,hook:`<p>📢 Context is a loudspeaker: every listener hears every announcement, even ones not meant for them.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> value = <span class="hljs-title function_">useMemo</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> ({ user, logout }), [user]); <span class="hljs-comment">// stable reference</span>
<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">AuthContext.Provider</span> <span class="hljs-attr">value</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{value}</span>></span>{children}<span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">AuthContext.Provider</span>></span></span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-prop-drilling`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Prop drilling: what is it, and how do you avoid it?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`props`,`composition`],explain:"Passing a prop through several components that don't use it, just to reach a deep child. Fix it with **composition** first (pass the deep child as `children` or an element prop), then Context, then a store for genuinely global data.",hook:`🚰 Don't run a pipe through every room; hand the thing directly to the room that needs it.`,example:"```jsx\n// Layout no longer needs to know about `user`\n<Layout sidebar={<UserMenu user={user} />} />\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Passing a prop through several components that don't use it, just to reach a deep child. Fix it with <strong>composition</strong> first (pass the deep child as <code>children</code> or an element prop), then Context, then a store for genuinely global data.</p>`,hook:`<p>🚰 Don't run a pipe through every room; hand the thing directly to the room that needs it.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-comment">// Layout no longer needs to know about \`user\`</span>
&#x3C;<span class="hljs-title class_">Layout</span> sidebar={<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">UserMenu</span> <span class="hljs-attr">user</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{user}</span> /></span></span>} />
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-refs`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Refs: what are they for? How do you pass one to a child?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`refs`,`dom`],explain:"A ref is either a handle to a DOM node or a mutable value that persists across renders **without causing one** (timer ids, latest values, previous props). In React 19, `ref` is an ordinary prop on function components; before that you needed `forwardRef`.",hook:`📌 A ref is a sticky note React never reads, so changing it never triggers a re-render.`,example:"```jsx\nfunction TextInput({ ref, ...props }) { return <input ref={ref} {...props} />; } // React 19\nconst inputRef = useRef(null);\n<TextInput ref={inputRef} />; inputRef.current?.focus();\n```",html:{explain:`<p>A ref is either a handle to a DOM node or a mutable value that persists across renders <strong>without causing one</strong> (timer ids, latest values, previous props). In React 19, <code>ref</code> is an ordinary prop on function components; before that you needed <code>forwardRef</code>.</p>`,hook:`<p>📌 A ref is a sticky note React never reads, so changing it never triggers a re-render.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">TextInput</span>(<span class="hljs-params">{ ref, ...props }</span>) { <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">input</span> <span class="hljs-attr">ref</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{ref}</span> {<span class="hljs-attr">...props</span>} /></span></span>; } <span class="hljs-comment">// React 19</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> inputRef = <span class="hljs-title function_">useRef</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">null</span>);
<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">TextInput</span> <span class="hljs-attr">ref</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{inputRef}</span> /></span></span>; inputRef.<span class="hljs-property">current</span>?.<span class="hljs-title function_">focus</span>();
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-error-boundaries`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What do error boundaries catch, and what do they miss?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`errors`],explain:`They catch errors thrown during rendering, in lifecycle methods and in constructors of the tree below them. They **don't** catch errors in event handlers (use try/catch), async code (a rejected promise in an effect), server-side rendering, or the boundary itself.`,hook:`🥅 An error boundary is a safety net under the render trapeze, not under the whole circus.`,html:{explain:`<p>They catch errors thrown during rendering, in lifecycle methods and in constructors of the tree below them. They <strong>don't</strong> catch errors in event handlers (use try/catch), async code (a rejected promise in an effect), server-side rendering, or the boundary itself.</p>`,hook:`<p>🥅 An error boundary is a safety net under the render trapeze, not under the whole circus.</p>`}},{id:`react-portals`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Portals: why use them?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`dom`,`modals`],explain:"`createPortal` renders children into a different DOM node (e.g. `document.body`), which lets modals and tooltips escape `overflow: hidden` and z-index stacking contexts. Events still bubble through the **React** tree, not the DOM tree.",hook:`🌀 A portal teleports the DOM, but the family tree (context, events) stays the same.`,html:{explain:`<p><code>createPortal</code> renders children into a different DOM node (e.g. <code>document.body</code>), which lets modals and tooltips escape <code>overflow: hidden</code> and z-index stacking contexts. Events still bubble through the <strong>React</strong> tree, not the DOM tree.</p>`,hook:`<p>🌀 A portal teleports the DOM, but the family tree (context, events) stays the same.</p>`}},{id:`react-code-splitting`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`How do you do code splitting in React?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`performance`,`bundling`],explain:"Use `React.lazy(() => import('./X'))` inside a `<Suspense fallback>` (or `next/dynamic`). Split by route first, then by heavy, rarely used components such as editors, charts and modals.",hook:`🧳 Pack the essentials; ship the heavy suitcase only when someone opens that door.`,example:"```jsx\nconst Chart = lazy(() => import('./Chart'));\n<Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}><Chart /></Suspense>\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Use <code>React.lazy(() => import('./X'))</code> inside a <code>&#x3C;Suspense fallback></code> (or <code>next/dynamic</code>). Split by route first, then by heavy, rarely used components such as editors, charts and modals.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧳 Pack the essentials; ship the heavy suitcase only when someone opens that door.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Chart</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_">lazy</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> <span class="hljs-keyword">import</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'./Chart'</span>));
<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Suspense</span> <span class="hljs-attr">fallback</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{</span>&#x3C;<span class="hljs-attr">Spinner</span> /></span>}><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Chart</span> /></span><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">Suspense</span>></span></span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-18-19`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What's new in React 18 and 19?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`react-19`,`concurrency`],explain:"**18:** concurrent rendering, automatic batching everywhere, `useTransition`/`useDeferredValue`, streaming SSR with Suspense. **19:** Actions (`useActionState`, `useFormStatus`, `useOptimistic`), the `use()` hook, `ref` as a plain prop, stable Server Components, and the **React Compiler**, which memoises automatically.",hook:`🧭 18 made React interruptible; 19 made forms, async and memoisation feel built-in.`,html:{explain:`<p><strong>18:</strong> concurrent rendering, automatic batching everywhere, <code>useTransition</code>/<code>useDeferredValue</code>, streaming SSR with Suspense. <strong>19:</strong> Actions (<code>useActionState</code>, <code>useFormStatus</code>, <code>useOptimistic</code>), the <code>use()</code> hook, <code>ref</code> as a plain prop, stable Server Components, and the <strong>React Compiler</strong>, which memoises automatically.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧭 18 made React interruptible; 19 made forms, async and memoisation feel built-in.</p>`}},{id:`react-transitions`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useTransition` vs `useDeferredValue`?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`concurrency`,`performance`],explain:"Both mark work as low priority so React can interrupt it for urgent input like typing. Use `useTransition` when **you own the state update** (wrap the setter, and get an `isPending` flag). Use `useDeferredValue` for a **value you receive** (from props or a store) when you can't wrap the update.",hook:`🎚️ Transition = "I'll mark my own update as slow"; deferred value = "I'll lag behind this value I was given".`,example:"```jsx\nconst [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();\nonChange={e => { setQuery(e.target.value); startTransition(() => setFilter(e.target.value)); }}\n\nconst deferred = useDeferredValue(query); // render the big list from `deferred`\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Both mark work as low priority so React can interrupt it for urgent input like typing. Use <code>useTransition</code> when <strong>you own the state update</strong> (wrap the setter, and get an <code>isPending</code> flag). Use <code>useDeferredValue</code> for a <strong>value you receive</strong> (from props or a store) when you can't wrap the update.</p>`,hook:`<p>🎚️ Transition = "I'll mark my own update as slow"; deferred value = "I'll lag behind this value I was given".</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useTransition</span>();
onChange={<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">e</span> =></span> { <span class="hljs-title function_">setQuery</span>(e.<span class="hljs-property">target</span>.<span class="hljs-property">value</span>); <span class="hljs-title function_">startTransition</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =></span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setFilter</span>(e.<span class="hljs-property">target</span>.<span class="hljs-property">value</span>)); }}

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> deferred = <span class="hljs-title function_">useDeferredValue</span>(query); <span class="hljs-comment">// render the big list from \`deferred\`</span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`react-uselayouteffect`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useLayoutEffect` vs `useEffect`?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`effects`,`layout`],explain:"`useLayoutEffect` runs after the DOM is updated but **before the browser paints**, and blocks the paint. Use it only to measure or adjust layout without flicker (tooltip positioning, scroll restoration). Everything else belongs in `useEffect`.",hook:`🎭 Layout effects fix the costume before the curtain rises; regular effects happen while the audience watches.`,html:{explain:`<p><code>useLayoutEffect</code> runs after the DOM is updated but <strong>before the browser paints</strong>, and blocks the paint. Use it only to measure or adjust layout without flicker (tooltip positioning, scroll restoration). Everything else belongs in <code>useEffect</code>.</p>`,hook:`<p>🎭 Layout effects fix the costume before the curtain rises; regular effects happen while the audience watches.</p>`}},{id:`react-hoc-render-props`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`HOCs vs render props vs hooks?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`patterns`,`reuse`],explain:`All three share logic between components. HOCs and render props wrap components, which leads to wrapper hell and unclear prop sources. Hooks replaced them for most cases. HOCs still show up for cross-cutting wrappers such as auth guards.`,hook:`🪆 HOCs and render props are nesting dolls; hooks let you just take the doll out.`,html:{explain:`<p>All three share logic between components. HOCs and render props wrap components, which leads to wrapper hell and unclear prop sources. Hooks replaced them for most cases. HOCs still show up for cross-cutting wrappers such as auth guards.</p>`,hook:`<p>🪆 HOCs and render props are nesting dolls; hooks let you just take the doll out.</p>`}}]};export{e as default};